Probability is a theory of modeling uncertainty in systems with which we interact.
One of the reasons it is a difficult subject to grasp is that the immutable mathematical object quantifying the likelihoods of all possible outcomes (i.e. a _measure_) is abstract and difficult to compare to actual outcomes it produces.
Subsequently, any concept related to uncertainty--no matter how simple it is to describe intuitively--must be defined in terms of measures and too must be quite technical and abstract.
To quickly build intuition, we will initially use the mathematical term _probability measure_ to simply identify an object which exhibits intuitive behavior that can be modeled in software without yet worrying about the technical definition.
From here, we will investigate the consequences of this functionality by building and running programs from it.
This will pave the way for us to frame our thinking in a _global_ perspective, where we understand probability measures as building-blocks we can compose to build larger hierarchical models.
Having this global perspective helps us segway into the actual mathematical definitions.

# Sampling

Let's consider the task of building a software library which allows us to simulate uncertain models.
We would like this library to be able to simulate the effects of anything from coin-flipping to poker hands or even the price of Costco stock in the next year.
The most natural way to model uncertainty in a computer program is for it produce values in a non-deterministic way.
From the perspective of a typed language, we would like to offer functionality for users to simulate seemingly random outcomes for all sorts of types--e.g. `bool`, `[Card; 5]`, or `Vec<f64>`.
Furthermore, the types `T` over which users may simulate random outcomes must be generic.
This process of randomly generating values `T` is referred to as _sampling_.

## Pseudorandom number generation

The simplest utility we can offer is that of a _pseudorandom number generator_ which acts as a source for users to randomly (at least seemingly) generate bytes for their type `T`.

<Statement {...rngDef} />

<Statement {...rngDefRemark} />

A true random number generator could be implemented by reading voltages from an analog-to-digital circuit, since quantum physics dictates that there is a true source of randomness present in the electronics.
Thankfully for us non-hardware guys, cryptography research requires psueodorandom number generation that behaves _very_ non-deterministically, so we can leverage some of their work.
Most of these implementations are hard to reason through, but only because they are highly optimized for hardware; in principle, they only typically involve performing simple addition and simple bitwise operations.
In this sequence, we will not worry about implementing `Rng` as all further objects will only interface with the trait.

## Probability measures

Returning to the task at hand, we define a probability measure (for now) as the object which serves our sampling desires.

